# The ledger of `jobs`: background work kept in a store and claimed by
# workers, measured against Go's github.com/riverqueue/river v0.51.2 (with
# `rivertype`) and github.com/hibiken/asynq v0.26.0 (their `go doc -all`,
# the modules fetched into the scratch folder on 2026-11-18), Python's
# Celery 5.7.0 and RQ 3.0.0 (`dir` of their applications, tasks, results,
# canvas, schedules, queues, jobs, workers and registries, installed into the
# scratch folder the same day), Ruby's Sidekiq 8.1.7 (the methods of its
# `Job`, `Client`, `Queue`, `JobRecord`, sets, `Stats` and process sets,
# installed into the scratch folder the same day; a `ruby` line), and Rust's
# apalis 1.2.0, apalis-core 1.2.0, apalis-sql 0.7.4, apalis-cron 1.1.0 and
# apalis-redis 1.1.1 (their items on docs.rs, read on 2026-11-19).
#
# The area is three faces: `jobs`, the values every store shares, and
# `jobs/postgres_store` and `jobs/redis_store`, which keep the jobs and
# answer the same calls. The blocks below hold the calls both stores answer,
# each naming both; the two stores' own ledgers hold what belongs to one of
# them. The expected values of the tests were asked of Python's croniter
# 7.0.0 (the runs of the schedules) and of the two servers themselves: the
# live runs, on 2026-11-19, reached PostgreSQL 16.15 (the image
# postgres:16.15-alpine, in a container of this Mac) and Redis 8.10.2 (built
# from its source in the scratch folder, run on this Mac's loopback).

capability jobs/job  a job as a value: which work, its arguments as JSON, its queue, its priority, its attempts
  heroes  jobs.Job, jobs.new_job, jobs.check_job, jobs.payload_text, jobs.DEFAULT_QUEUE, jobs.DEFAULT_PRIORITY, jobs.FIRST_PRIORITY, jobs.LAST_PRIORITY, jobs.DEFAULT_MAX_ATTEMPTS, jobs.MAX_NAME_BYTES
  go      river.JobArgs, river.Job, river.InsertOpts (Queue, Priority, MaxAttempts), river.InsertManyParams, river.JobArgsWithInsertOpts, river.JobArgsWithKindAliases, river.Config.MaxAttempts, rivertype.JobArgs, rivertype.JobInsertParams, asynq.NewTask, asynq.Task, asynq.Task.Type, asynq.Task.Payload, asynq.Option, asynq.OptionType, asynq.Queue, asynq.MaxRetry
  rust    apalis::prelude::Task, apalis::prelude::TaskBuilder, apalis::prelude::Data, apalis::prelude::TaskFn, apalis::prelude::task_fn, apalis_core::task::Task, apalis_core::task::builder::TaskBuilder, apalis_core::task::data::Data, apalis_core::task::data::AddExtension, apalis_core::task::data::MissingDataError, apalis_core::task::task_fn::TaskFn, apalis_core::task::task_fn::task_fn, apalis_core::task::from_request::FromRequest, apalis_core::task::into_response::IntoResponse, apalis_core::backend::codec::Codec
  python  celery.Task, celery.Task.name, celery.Task.priority, celery.Task.max_retries, celery.Task.apply_async (queue, priority, max_retries), celery.Task.signature, celery.Signature, celery.signature, celery.shared_task, celery.Celery.task, celery.Celery.register_task, celery.Celery.tasks, rq.job.Job.create, rq.job.Job.args, rq.job.Job.kwargs, rq.job.Job.func, rq.job.Job.func_name, rq.job.Job.instance, rq.job.Job.data, rq.Queue.create_job, rq.Queue.parse_args
  ruby    Sidekiq::Job.set, Sidekiq::Job::Setter.set, Sidekiq::Job.sidekiq_options, Sidekiq::Job.queue_as, Sidekiq.default_job_options
  note    the kind is the program's own name for the work, as river's `Kind`, asynq's type and Celery's task name are, and the payload any JSON value; a priority from 1, claimed first, to 9, 5 by default, so a job can be put before the rest or after them: river's runs 1 to 4 with 1 the default, Celery's on Redis 0 to 9, and asynq, RQ and Sidekiq have none per job

